// theater.templ — C-CALV4-THEATER (slice R2-3). The scaffold and the opener.
//
// The producer half — the seed, the copy, the re-namespace and the two ids —
// is theater.go, and its header carries the reasoning. This file is the markup
// and the three placement constraints [TH-14] composes.

package calendar

import (
	"github.com/keyxmakerx/chronicle/internal/templates/layouts"
	calblock "github.com/keyxmakerx/chronicle/internal/widgets/calendar_block"
)

// theaterOpener is the `Expand` control ([TH-9] SIGNED).
//
// A REAL <button>, BESIDE THE EXISTING ANCHOR, NOT INSTEAD OF IT. The embed's
// header already carries `Open full calendar`; re-pointing that anchor is
// R2-4's (the V2 sunset) and this slice does not touch it.
//
// LABELLED `Expand`, NOT `Full calendar`, AND THAT IS A RULING RATHER THAN A
// PREFERENCE. Full tier is `@container cal-block (min-width: 900px)` and NO
// phone viewport reaches it, so on a phone the theater is a std-tier Block with
// its zones STACKED. That is real depth, deliberately opened — but "full" would
// be false on a 390px screen and the affordance must not promise a tier the
// width cannot deliver.
//
// EMITTED FOR EVERY VIEWER WHO RECEIVES THE EMBED, INCLUDING ANONYMOUS. The
// theater shows what the viewer's own projection already contains and nothing
// else, so there is no permission difference here for a role-varying control to
// signal — and a control present for a GM and absent for a Player would INVENT
// one, which is the inverse of the arc's absence rule and just as misleading.
//
// NOT SUPPRESSED BELOW A WIDTH ([TH-7] SIGNED), twice over: the theater is a
// genuine feature on a phone, and a server-rendered attribute cannot vary by
// viewport (ADR-048 §13 — the bound this arc has now lost to twice).
//
// GUARD B3: the control's data attribute ends in `-pick` and reuses none of
// STATE_MARKERS' six nouns.
templ theaterOpener(scaffoldID string) {
	<button
		type="button"
		class="text-xs text-accent hover:underline"
		data-theater-pick={ scaffoldID }
		aria-haspopup="dialog"
		aria-expanded="false"
		aria-controls={ scaffoldID }
		title="Expand this calendar over the page"
	>
		<i class="fa-solid fa-up-right-and-down-left-from-center mr-1" aria-hidden="true"></i>Expand
	</button>
}

// theaterScaffold is the theater itself: a top-layer `<dialog>` carrying a
// SECOND render of the Block this page already built.
//
// ── WHY A <dialog>, AND WHY IT IS A NAMED DIVERGENCE ([TH-1] SIGNED) ────────
//
// The day card chose `popover="manual"` and said why: `auto` closes
// synchronously through the UA and the hiding `beforetoggle` is not cancelable,
// so the register's 160ms close would be skipped on every path but the button.
// That reasoning is sound and it applies here — but a 1180px overlay with a
// month grid, a Ledger and a Shelf in it has dozens of focusable descendants
// and a whole entity page behind them, and modal scale brings three
// requirements a 340px card did not have: focus CONTAINMENT, INERTNESS of what
// is behind, and a CANCELABLE escape. `<dialog>.showModal()` gives the first
// two natively, and its `cancel` event IS cancelable — so the register survives
// on every path INCLUDING the one the UA owns, which is strictly better than
// the manual-popover workaround. The card stays exactly as it is; this is not a
// retrofit of R2-2a and no file of its is opened.
//
// ── WHERE THIS SITS, AND WHY ([TH-14], RE-SIGNED 2026-08-08) ────────────────
//
// Three constraints compose here and all three bind at once.
//
//  1. OUTSIDE every `.cal-block-host`. `.cal-block-host .block` is
//     `overflow: hidden` (calendar-block.css:503), so a box positioned inside
//     the Block is CLIPPED — the same fact that rules out a page section and
//     forces the top layer, and it does not stop being true because the
//     element is later promoted. The scaffold is a SIBLING of the embed's
//     Block mount, never a child of it.
//
//  2. INSIDE a `cal-bench` root — and here the dialog IS that root, carrying
//     `cal-bench` as a SECOND SCOPE ROOT the way schedule.templ carries
//     `class="cal-bench cal-schedule"`. TOP-LAYER PROMOTION DOES NOT CHANGE
//     DOM ANCESTRY, so a `<dialog>` mounted outside `.cal-bench` would get no
//     register at all; carrying the class on the element itself makes the
//     scope independent of where the mount happens to land. bench.templ:104
//     says this in the code's own words about the day card, and it is why that
//     comment exists. The class name is a small lie — a theater on an entity
//     page is not a Bench — and it is the same lie `/schedule` already tells;
//     the product prefers it to a second disclosure register, which [DC-6]
//     SIGNED calls laundering by name.
//
//  3. THE THIRD CONSTRAINT WAS `OUTSIDE ANY HTMX-SWAPPABLE REGION`, AND IT WAS
//     REPLACED BY THE 2026-08-08 RE-SIGN. The original cited
//     `widgetbindings.BindingAffordance` as the swappable region. That premise
//     is REFUTED: `calendar_widget_type.go:152` wraps the ENTIRE output of
//     EntityCalendarBlock in `widgetbindings.BlockHost`, and
//     `internal/plugins/widgetbindings/picker.templ` targets THAT wrapper with
//     `hx-swap="outerHTML"` on three live paths (bind, create, unbind). The
//     swappable region is the whole component, one level ABOVE anything this
//     file can emit, so NO position reachable from this slice satisfied the
//     constraint as written. The build stopped there, correctly, and the block
//     was wrong rather than the builder.
//
//     SO THE SCAFFOLD SITS INSIDE THE SWAPPABLE REGION, DELIBERATELY. The
//     failure that constraint was written to prevent — an opener that outlives
//     its scaffold, "opens once and then stops opening, with every guard
//     green" — requires the two to be in DIFFERENT subtrees. Here they are in
//     the SAME one: the swap replaces the whole component, so opener and
//     scaffold die and are reborn together, and calendar_theater.js re-binds
//     on `htmx:afterSettle` ([TH-12]) with an idempotent wiring guard. There
//     is no state in which a live opener points at a dead scaffold.
//
//     THE ONE REAL RISK IS A DIALOG THAT IS OPEN WHEN ITS SUBTREE IS SWAPPED —
//     removed from the DOM mid-modal, stranding the top layer. Narrow (while
//     the theater is open the picker behind it is inert, so the swap must be
//     driven from elsewhere) but real. ITS REQUIRED COUNTERPART is the
//     module's `htmx:beforeSwap` listener, which closes the theater when the
//     swap target contains it — pinned by a test that swaps the host with the
//     theater open and asserts the dialog closed and focus returned.
//
//     Both alternatives stay available and neither is this slice's: emitting
//     the scaffold outside `BlockHost` is a `widgetbindings` restructure —
//     another plugin's surface — and emitting it page-level (the day card's
//     precedent) crosses into the entity page's own template. Moving it later
//     RETIRES the `beforeSwap` guard rather than fighting it.
//
// ── THE HAZARD THE NEXT SLICE WILL BREAK ON (dispatch §12) ──────────────────
//
// THIS PAGE NOW CARRIES TWO BLOCKS FOR THE SAME CALENDAR, so `dayKey` produces
// the SAME `data-day` value on both. Nothing consumes it on an entity page
// today — the day card is Bench-only and this scaffold deliberately emits none
// of its three markers — but a future host that mounts the card AND the theater
// together MUST scope its queries to one Block. That is the single most likely
// way a later slice breaks on this work.
//
// AND THE THREE MARKERS ARE BANNED HERE BY NAME ([TH-1]). `calendar_daycard.js`
// rides the same body-script registry this module does, so it is ALREADY LOADED
// on every entity page; it is dormant only because its `init()` requires both
// `[data-cal-daycard]` and `[data-cal-daycard-payload]`, and its `cellFrom`
// resolves a Block by `closest('[data-bench-block]')`. Emitting any one of the
// three would wake the card on a page that then holds two Blocks with two
// different Ledger states, silently re-opening the SIGNED STOP-AND-FLAG
// C-CALV4-CARD-CROSSBLOCK-LEDGER on a surface it was never measured against.
// Wiring the card into the theater is C-CALV4-THEATER-DAYCARD and is entered by
// CHOICE, in its own slice, with its own geometry probe.
//
// ── THE SWITCHBOARD IS ABSENT, NOT HIDDEN ([TH-13]) ─────────────────────────
//
// The Block below renders with `HasSwitchboard=false` and an empty
// `PersistURL`, both cleared on the COPY in theater.go, so the widget emits no
// `[popover]`, no `⋯` invoker and no layer sheet at all. The EMBED keeps its
// switchboard and remains the one place a viewer says "I want the Ledger
// permanently".
templ theaterScaffold(scaffoldID, headingID, title string, block calblock.BlockData) {
	// The register's home. [TH-3] SIGNED: the theater carries `cal-bench` as a
	// second scope root and the entity page links the Bench's sheet, because
	// there is EXACTLY ONE disclosure register in the product and a second
	// sheet with its own `--disc-*` is laundering. The theater's OWN sheet
	// declares no transition, no @keyframes and no `--disc-*`; its two motion
	// rules live BY NAME inside the one register section of calendar-bench.css,
	// exactly as the day card's do.
	<link rel="stylesheet" href={ layouts.AssetURL("/static/css/calendar-bench.css") }/>
	<link rel="stylesheet" href={ layouts.AssetURL("/static/css/calendar-theater.css") }/>
	<dialog
		id={ scaffoldID }
		class="cal-bench cal-theater"
		data-cal-theater
		aria-modal="true"
		aria-labelledby={ headingID }
	>
		// .tbox is the REVEAL's subject and it carries no clamp of its own.
		// The register animates its `block-size`, and [TH-16] is explicit that
		// a scroll container or a max-block-size placed HERE would make the
		// reveal animate against a clamped box — the transition either does not
		// run at all or runs to the wrong size, with every guard still green.
		// The scroll container is the content region INSIDE it.
		<div class="tbox" data-theater-box>
			<div class="thead">
				<h2 class="thd" id={ headingID }>{ title }</h2>
				<button
					type="button"
					class="tclose"
					data-theater-close
					aria-label="Close"
					title="Close"
				>
					<i class="fa-solid fa-xmark" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				</button>
			</div>
			// THE CONTENT REGION — the theater's scroll container ([TH-16]).
			// Never `.cal-block-host`: that is the container-query container,
			// and scrolling or clamping it is a tier hazard dressed as a layout
			// choice. The header above sits OUTSIDE it, which is what keeps the
			// close control reachable without scrolling at the 768px floor and
			// on a phone.
			<div class="tbody" data-theater-scroll>
				@calblock.Block(block)
			</div>
		</div>
	</dialog>
}
